<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<worksheet xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Logistic Growth</title>

  <exercise>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Let's look at the logistic equation
        <md>
          \frac{dy}{dt} = \frac{1}{10}y(8-y).
        </md>
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Sketch a graph of <m>dy/dt</m> as a
          function of <m>y</m>.  Then sketch a
          rough slope field.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="36">
        <image width="40%">
          <xi:include href="prefigure/36-graph-1.xml"/>
        </image>
        <image width="40%">
          <xi:include href="prefigure/36-slope-1.xml"/>
        </image>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Identify any equilibrium solutions and
          determine if they are stable or unstable.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="36">
        <p>
          There is an unstable equilibrium at <m>y = 0</m> and a
          stable equilibrium at <m>y = 8</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          What do you predict happens after a long
          time if <m>y(0) = 1</m>?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="36">
        <p>
          The slope field shows us that
          <m>y \to 8</m> as <m>t \to \infty</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Write the solution satisfying
          <m>y(0) = 1</m>.  Sketch a graph of
          <m>y(t)</m> and verify your prediction.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="36">
        <p>
          Separating variables and using partial
          fractions
          <md>
            <mrow>
              \int\frac{1}{y(8-y)}\,dy
              \amp= \int\frac{1}{10}\,dt
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \frac{1}{8}\ln\!\left|\frac{y}{8-y}
              \right| \amp= \frac{t}{10}+C
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \frac{y}{8-y} \amp= Ae^{4t/5}
            </mrow>
          </md>.
          Using <m>y(0)=1</m>, we see that <m>A = 1/7</m>,
          so
          <md>
            y(t) = \frac{8}{1+7e^{-4t/5}}.
          </md>
          As <m>t\to\infty</m>,
          <m>y \to 8</m>.
        </p>
        <image width="40%">
          <xi:include href="prefigure/36-sol-1.xml"/>
        </image>
      </solution>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Recently, we modeled the Earth's population
        with a logistic equation with
        <m>k = 0.0025</m> and <m>N = 12</m>
        billion.
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Write the logistic differential equation.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="36">
        <p>
          <m>\frac{dP}{dt} = 0.0025\,P(12-P)</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          The population is currently close to
          <m>P = 8</m> billion.  What does our
          equation predict for the rate of change
          of the population?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="36">
        <p>
          <m>dP/dt = 0.0025(8)(12-8) =
          0.0025(8)(4) = 0.08</m> billion/yr,
          or about 80 million people per year.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Write the function <m>P(t)</m> assuming
          that <m>t = 0</m> corresponds to the year
          2020 with a population of 8 billion.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="36">
        <p>
          Using the logistic formula with
          <m>N = 12</m>, <m>P_0 = 8</m>,
          <m>kN = 0.03</m>:
          <md>
            P(t) = \frac{12}{1 + \frac12
            e^{-0.03t}}.
          </md>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          What does this predict for the population
          in 2030?  2050?  2100?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="36">
        <p>
          <ul>
            <li>
              2030 (<m>t=10</m>):
              <m>12/(1 + 0.5e^{-0.3})
              \approx 8.76</m> billion.
            </li>
            <li>
              2050 (<m>t=30</m>):
              <m>12/(1 + 0.5e^{-0.9})
              \approx 9.97</m> billion.
            </li>
            <li>
              2100 (<m>t=80</m>):
              <m>12/(1 + 0.5e^{-2.4})
              \approx 11.5</m> billion.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          When will the population reach
          10 billion?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="36">
        <p>
          Setting <m>P(t) = 10</m>:
          <md>
            <mrow>
              1 + \tfrac12 e^{-0.03t}
              \amp= \tfrac{12}{10} = 1.2
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              e^{-0.03t} \amp= 0.4
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              t \amp= \frac{\ln(2.5)}{0.03}
              \approx 30.5
            </mrow>
          </md>
          years after 2020, i.e., around 2050.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Suppose that <m>P(t)</m> measures the
        number of bacteria living in a colony in a
        Petri dish, where <m>P</m> is measured in
        thousands of bacteria and <m>t</m> is
        measured in days.
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace="0.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          One day, you measure that there are
          6 thousand bacteria and the rate of
          change is 18 thousand bacteria per day.
          What is the per capita growth rate?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="36">
        <p>
          Per capita rate
          <m>= (dP/dt)/P = 18/6 = 3</m>
          per day.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0.7in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          A while later, you measure that there are
          9 thousand bacteria and the rate of
          change is still 18 thousand bacteria per
          day.
        </p>
        <p>
          Using this data, write the per capita
          growth rate as a linear function of the
          population; that is, find <m>k</m> and
          <m>N</m> so that the per capita growth
          rate is <m>k(N-P)</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="36">
        <p>
          The per capita growth rate at <m>P=9</m> is <m>18/9 =
          2</m>. So we need
          <md>
            <mrow>
              k(N-6) \amp = 3
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              k(N-9) \amp = 2
            </mrow>
          </md>.
          The slope is <m>-\frac13</m> so <m>k=\frac13</m>.  Then
          <md>
            \frac13(N-6) = 3
          </md>
          so that <m>N=15</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Assuming the population grows according to a logistic model,
          write a differential equation for
          <m>dP/dt</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="36">
        <p>
          <m>\frac{dP}{dt} =
          \frac{1}{3}(15-P)P</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          What will happen to the bacteria
          population after a very long time?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="36">
        <p>
          As <m>t</m> grows, <m>P</m> will converge to the stable
          equilibrium, <m>P \to 15</m> thousand (the
          carrying capacity).
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          At what population is the number of
          bacteria increasing most rapidly?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="36">
        <image width="40%">
          <xi:include href="prefigure/36-graph-2.xml"/>
        </image>
        <p>
          The graph shows us that the rate is greatest when
          <m>P = N/2 = 15/2 = 7.5</m> thousand.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          If the initial population is 6 thousand,
          how long does it take for the population
          to reach 12 thousand?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="36">
        <p>
          With <m>P_0 = 6</m>, <m>N = 15</m>,
          <m>kN = 5</m>, we have
          <md>
            P(t) = \frac{15}{1 + \frac{3}{2}
            e^{-5t}}.
          </md>
          Setting <m>P = 12</m>, we see that
          <m>1 + \frac32 e^{-5t} = \frac{5}{4}</m>,
          so <m>e^{-5t} = \frac{1}{6}</m> and
          <md>
            t = \frac{\ln 6}{5} \approx 0.36
          </md>
          days.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
  </exercise>

</worksheet>
